<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd al-Hafid of Morocco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mulai Hafid, Abdelhafid of Morocco</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd al-Hafid was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaouite-dynasty">Alaouite dynasty</a> from 1908 to 1912. He rebelled against his brother Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a>, whose concessions to European powers were unpopular, and was proclaimed sultan in 1907–1908. Unable to control revolt or resist foreign pressure, he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fez">Treaty of Fez</a> on 30 March 1912, making Morocco a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, and abdicated later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-hafid-of-morocco.jpg" alt="Abd al-Hafid of Morocco" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J. Giry, Rue de Nimes 115, Vichy (France) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%84%D8%B7%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D9%81%D9%8A%D8%B8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
